// @check
// Soundness corpus index (ROADMAP A12).
//
// This directory pins down VL's type-safety contract: every well-typed program
// is type-safe at runtime (see docs/soundness.md). Each file is a small, focused
// "must-error" or "must-not-error" program so a future deep change to inference,
// narrowing, or codegen cannot silently regress soundness — a regression flips a
// must-not-error case into an error (or vice versa) and breaks `deno task test`.
//
// The runner is STRICT BY DEFAULT (see tests/cases_test.ts): a test fails on any error
// or warning it did not declare. So the two halves of the contract are:
//   // @check / @run with NO @error  — the "must-not-error" half: the file is
//             asserting it compiles with zero diagnostics (@check = not run,
//             @run = also runs and checks `@log` output end to end). No @ok
//             directive is needed — clean compile is the default.
//   // @error / @error-at            — the "must-error" half: an unsound use is
//             rejected with a diagnostic containing the given text.
//
// NAMING. A case's name says what it PINS, and the runner's verdict is the only
// thing that says whether it passes. Those are different axes, and conflating
// them has cost real time: six `xfail-*` files here were ordinary passing @error
// pins wearing a name that read "known failure", which is why ROADMAP A13 read
// OPEN long after its defect was fixed. So:
//   `*-reject.vl`  — a must-error pin. The @error is the DESIRED verdict: an
//             unsound (or otherwise illegal) program that the checker correctly
//             refuses. `arith-hole-operand-reject.vl`,
//             `generic-fn-param-mismatch-reject.vl`.
//   `*-sound.vl` / plain — a must-not-error pin, per the two halves above.
//   `xfail-*.vl`   — the compiler is WRONG here, and the file pins the wrong
//             behavior so that fixing it is loud instead of silent.
//
// `xfail-` alone is ambiguous, because a checker can be wrong in two opposite
// directions. The prefix therefore always carries which one:
//   `xfail-unsound-*.vl`      — ACCEPTS TOO MUCH. A program that should be
//             refused compiles clean (no @error, a `TODO(soundness):` note). The
//             gap is a hole in the type-safety contract. When it closes, the file
//             becomes an ordinary `*-reject.vl` @error pin — `array-element-
//             recursion.vl` and `arith-hole-operand-reject.vl` both made that trip.
//             There are currently NONE: the live unsound corner (container element
//             variance, `Cat[]` into `Animal[]`) is not pinned here at all — see
//             docs/internals/open-rulings.md.
//   `xfail-false-reject-*.vl` — REJECTS TOO MUCH. A SOUND program is refused, so
//             the file carries an @error pinning the UNDESIRED diagnostic. This is
//             a completeness gap, never a soundness one; closing it LOOSENS the
//             checker, which makes it reject-parity work. When it closes, drop the
//             prefix and flip the file to @run + @log.
//   `xfail-miscompile-*.vl`   — ACCEPTS, THEN MISCOMPILES. A well-typed program
//             the checker is RIGHT to accept, which the emitter then lowers wrong:
//             `vl check --codegen` exits 0 and the module does not instantiate (or
//             the compiler crashes emitting it). Neither of the two above fits —
//             the program is not illegal, so it is not "accepts too much", and it
//             is not refused, so it is not "rejects too much". The failure is in
//             codegen, not in the type-safety contract.
//             SEVEN more were found and FIXED during the band-1
//             destringify programme (six check-clean invalid-wasm holes plus one
//             out-of-bounds compiler crash) and every one was FIXED in the same
//             change that found it, so each is pinned as an ordinary `@run` or
//             `@emit-error` case under tests/cases/generics/ instead. The prefix
//             exists for the one that cannot be fixed immediately — a defect of
//             this shape has no other home, which is why `docs/internals/
//             destringify-types-program.md` had to carry B9 in prose for four
//             slices before it was closed.
//             The channel that SEES this kind is `vl build` / `vl run` (they
//             instantiate); `vl check`, even `--codegen`, does not. The directive
//             is `@no-instantiate` — added WITH this kind, because the harness had
//             nothing that could pin it: `@trap` asserts a `VLRuntimeError` from a
//             module that DID load and explicitly rejects the CompileError a
//             miscompile produces. A bare `@no-instantiate` accepts any
//             instantiation failure; add TEXT to match the engine's message.
//             The kind's first member — `xfail-miscompile-permuted-struct-fields.vl`,
//             two field-permuted object types that were one type to the checker (the
//             2026-08 ruling) and two rows to the emitter — is CLOSED: the ruling was
//             carried into the emitter, and the file made this kind's documented trip,
//             flipping to `@run` as `tests/cases/types/permuted-named-alias-shares-row.vl`.
//             There are FIVE members now, and the atom-widening family that briefly held four of
//             them is CLOSED. Its shape is worth keeping. A literal union that reached the i32
//             ATOM rep was not widened back to a string at several sinks, and the four positions
//             looked like one defect because they shared a symptom — an i32 where a reference was
//             wanted. They were two defects. THREE were VALUE sinks (the `string[]` push, the
//             `string | null` return, the `(string | null)[]` push), each fixed by asking for a
//             widen that already existed, each now an @run case under tests/cases/literal-unions/.
//             The FOURTH was a REP DISAGREEMENT: a NARROWED literal-union PARAMETER is ALREADY
//             declared `i32`, and the predicates deciding whether to treat it as an atom asked
//             the wrong question — so `print(v)` emitted the raw id, a `: string` return lowered
//             the id into a reference, and the CALLER lowered its member literal as a string into
//             the i32 slot. Three symptoms, one wrong question. Fixed as
//             tests/cases/literal-unions/narrowed-litunion-param-atom-rep.vl by asking
//             `tyLitUnionAliasIx`, which consults the checker's COPY-PROVENANCE table as well as
//             the alias registry and is what the REP LAYER already uses to assign that valtype,
//             instead of the registry-only `nodeTyIsLitUnionAlias`. A STRUCTURAL test would have
//             been wrong and was tried first: it claims the atom rep for a bare inline
//             `"ia" | "ib"` too, which canon-softens to a string reference, so it swaps one
//             miscompile for another. That fixture pins the control alongside the fix.
//             "CLOSED" MEANS THE FOUR FILED POSITIONS, NOT THE SHAPE. Three narrowed-param
//             shapes remain broken and two are pinned here: a FUNCTION-VALUE call
//             (`xfail-miscompile-narrowed-litunion-fn-value-arg.vl`, whose `$fnsig` is built from
//             a rendered spelling that a narrowed copy has no declared name for) and the shadowed
//             binding below. The third is a GENERIC instantiation — `id<T>(v: T)` called with a
//             narrowed value — which fails identically on both sides of the fix and is not pinned
//             here yet. The CAPTURE channel was a fourth and is now fixed, but only after an
//             intermediate build that moved the direct call without it turned a loud reject into
//             a silently WRONG printed value; that is why
//             tests/cases/literal-unions/narrowed-litunion-param-atom-rep.vl carries capture arms
//             and states its reader count.
//             THE "FOR UNSHADOWED BINDINGS" QUALIFIER those closures carried is now DISCHARGED.
//             The scan/emit name-resolution hole — the reservation scan resolving a shadowed name
//             to the OUTER binding while the emit resolves it to the INNER one, leaving the
//             string-op scratch frame unreserved — is closed by a scan-side over-approximation,
//             and `xfail-miscompile-shadowed-atom-widen-unreserved-frame.vl` made the trip to
//             `@run` as tests/cases/literal-unions/shadowed-atom-widen-reserves-frame.vl. Closing
//             it took two passes: the first enumerated the six `*WidensAtomToStr` predicates and
//             missed three more scan-side askers of the SAME question — `print`, an object-literal
//             field value, and an if-arm join — each still emitting invalid wasm at `vl check`
//             rc 0. ENUMERATE THE QUESTION, NOT THE PREDICATES THAT ASK IT.
//             A SEPARATE shadowing defect survives here and is not that one:
//             `xfail-miscompile-local-shadowing-param-ignored.vl`, where codegen resolves a local
//             shadowing a PARAMETER to the parameter while the CHECKER binds it correctly — a
//             checker/codegen split rather than a reservation gap, and the seventh member.
//             The family was found by ADVERSARIAL REVIEW rather than by any sweep, which keeps
//             the tally at six of seven surviving members found by review. The fourth,
//             `xfail-miscompile-prim-named-type-alias.vl`, is a `type` declaration taking a
//             PRIMITIVE's name (`type f64 = "a" | "b"`): the checker resolves that name through
//             `primTyOfName` FIRST while the emitter's alias registry takes it verbatim, so the
//             two disagree and a value is stored as an f64 and read as an atom box. Every prim
//             name reproduces it. ALSO found by a reviewer — refuting a claim about what a
//             producer can render required constructing one, and it turned out to be both
//             accepted and broken. Three of the four members were found by review rather than by
//             a sweep, which is worth knowing when deciding where to spend the next hour.
//             The third,
//             `xfail-miscompile-newtype-string-inferred-return.vl`, is an UN-ANNOTATED return of
//             a newtype over STRING: the inferred-return classifier does not erase the brand, so
//             the result valtype falls to the i32 default while the body returns a string ref.
//             Scoped by two controls under tests/cases/inference/ — the ANNOTATED string form and
//             the un-annotated f64 form both work, so only the ref-valtype base miscompiles.
//             Also found by a REVIEWER, this time while refuting a by-construction claim about
//             how a branded `TyPrim` renders. The second,
//             `xfail-miscompile-permuted-object-closure-arms.vl`, is two closure arms whose
//             PARAMETER object types are field-permuted: one type under the 2026-08 ruling,
//             so one signature, and the union box lowers with a nullability mismatch
//             (`expected (ref null $type), found (ref $type)`). It was found by a REVIEWER
//             checking whether an unrelated change moved that partition — the shape was
//             already broken — which is worth noting as a discovery channel: neither the
//             corpus nor any sweep had reached it.
//             The first is
//             `xfail-miscompile-toplevel-nested-array-inferred-empty.vl`, a TOP-LEVEL
//             inferred empty `[]` whose element a `.push` pins to an ARRAY. Its
//             IN-FUNCTION form is fixed and pinned under tests/cases/arrays/; the fix
//             lives in `collectLocals`, which types wasm LOCALS and never sees a global,
//             so extending it is a different mechanism rather than a wider guard. It is
//             pinned rather than fixed for that reason, and because the OTHER element
//             kinds at top level reject CLEANLY — only this one miscompiles, which is
//             what keeps the residue narrow enough to pin honestly.
// Renaming across these is not cosmetic — the name is what a reader (and the
// ROADMAP) uses to decide whether an item is still open.
//
// This file is itself a trivial must-not-error case so the directory is never
// empty. `_corpus` is intentionally unread (leading `_`) -> a faded hint, not a
// warning (asserted below to keep the strict harness green).
// @hint Intentionally-unused variable `_corpus`
let _corpus = 0
